2. /usr/local/bin/awg -- GNU General Public License, version 2
--------------------------------------------------------------

amneziawg-tools 3.1.20260812, a fork of wireguard-tools, built from upstream's
own release tag by tools/build-awg-freebsd.sh in the source repository.

Earlier releases of this package shipped the binary from the FreeBSD port
net/amnezia-tools untouched. That stopped being possible at AmneziaWG 3.x: the
port is still at 1.0.20250903, and no FreeBSD binary of 3.x is published
anywhere.

It carries one modification, and only one: the build defines
AWG_NO_KERNEL_IPC, which leaves out src/ipc-freebsd.h. That file does not
compile against 3.x -- it is written against the older containers.h, where
H1-H4 were strings -- and it is the path that talks to the if_amn kernel
module, which pfSense cannot load. Everything this package does goes through
the userspace path instead. The change is one line of sed in the build script,
which is part of the corresponding source described at the end of this file.

    Copyright (C) 2015-2021 Jason A. Donenfeld <Jason@zx2c4.com>
    Copyright (C) 2018-2021 WireGuard LLC
    and the other holders named in the source

It is a separate program: this package executes it, it is not linked into
anything and nothing here derives from it. Bundling it in the same archive is
mere aggregation under section 2 of the GPL, which is why the rest of this
package can be Apache 2.0. What the GPL does require is this notice, the copy
of the licence next to it, and the corresponding source -- see the end of this
file.


3. /usr/local/bin/amneziawg-go -- MIT
-------------------------------------

amneziawg-go 3.1.20260814, a fork of wireguard-go. Built from source with the
FreeBSD sticky-sockets patch that lives in patches/amneziawg-go/ in the source
repository, which is why `amneziawg-go --version` reports -sticky1. Without it
a tunnel published on a WAN that does not hold the default route never
completes a handshake; the patch is upstream as amneziawg-go#180.

The binary links golang.org/x/crypto, golang.org/x/net and golang.org/x/sys,
all BSD-3-Clause (see `go version -m`). Its go.mod names more -- the Outline
SDK, gvisor, quic-go -- but none of them end up in this build.

